{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1101620-m20-worth-it-for-my-game","id":1101620,"title":"M20: Worth it for my Game?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-08T06:51:50-07:00","id":1101620,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> M20: Worth it for my Game?","content":"It looks like my group's current game is ending, and I find myself with an opportunity to maybe, possibly, finally introduce them to Mage. Currently I have a few copies of Mage Revised, and I'm not sure if I should invest in M20, and ask my players to do the same.<br /> <br /> A few things about the game I'm planning to run:<ul><li>I'm dumping about 90% of the fluff around the Traditions, Crafts, and the Technocracy. I'm replacing it with my own Mage groups, some of which draw vague inspiration from canon.</li> <li>I'm drastically changing the overall metaplot, for example the Ascension War simply never happened. The Council exists to exercise power over the supernatural world as a whole.</li> <li>I'm 100% sold on doing the above.</li> </ul>With that in mind, does M20 have anything to offer me that Mage Revised does not? I would be most interested in cleaner or clearer explanations for magick and useful NPCs, but general opinions are welcome.<br /> <br /> And If M20 happens to be controversial on the whole on this particular forum, I humbly ask that this thread not become a venue for that controversy. This is my first time engaging with the online Mage community in about a decade; please be gentle. Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T07:08:03-07:00","id":1101628,"title":"","content":"M20 has a lot of useful stuff about Paradigm, and many side-rules / alternate rules that are very interesting. It gives a lot of posibilities.<br /> For instance, the rules of Reality Zones (areas where what's Coincident and what's Vulgar shift according to a local Paradigm), the rules for breaking stuff. It also gives expanded rules for Rituals and new rules for Paradigm that are both very good and clear.<br /> M20 rules are much more benevolent than Revised in regards at what a mage can accomplish on a single (or a few) turns, but less benevolent with Rituals. Which are two gains as far as I'm concerned, but that's a personal preference.<br /> <br /> Revised, however, explains MUCH better how to work magick with the Spheres, and what the limmits should be.<br /> If you're interested in &quot;cleaner or clearer explanations for magick&quot; you won't find them in M20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 06-08-2017, 10:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T08:38:32-07:00","id":1101652,"title":"","content":"One of the strengths of Mage 20 is that you get a pretty nice comparison of the way Revised rules work against previous editions, and ways to incorporate/discard metaplot as suits your purposes. The latter may be less useful to you since you're revamping your setting. On the other hand, it helps break up the metaplot elements into individual components, which makes it easier to determine which parts, if any, you might want to keep. There isn't anything by way of NPCs though, sorry.<br /> <br /> I would recommend it for new Focus rules alone. I found it very helpful in terms of helping me and my players around the conceptual use of magic, beyond just &quot;I'm using Forces 3, Matter 2&quot;. Paradigms and practices are much more clearly defined, and I really like the changes in the instrument rules (basically having a number of general or personal instruments rather than one specific tool/focus for one specific sphere.)<br /> <br /> There are also a lot of gameplay charts: magic, combat, et cetera and so forth, which can be really helpful for quick reference. (Especially if you're using the pdf, since there is a very thorough table of contents and internal hyperlinks.)<br /> <br /> <b>Aleph</b>'s right about the sphere write ups though. They're very short in M20. (Probably because of the How Do You Do That? supplement.) If you're specifically looking for comprehensive sphere write-ups, I'd suggest looking at the Sorcerer's Crusade core rule book. I've always thought it had the clearest and most comprehensive write ups. (And some very nice charts to boot. I'm a big fan of charts. :-))","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 06-08-2017, 11:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T10:09:46-07:00","id":1101671,"title":"","content":"Oddly, I actually like the M20 approach to explaining the Spheres more. Both M20 and the previous editions do the same basic thing: a paragraph that explains the essential idea of what each dot of each Sphere does. In previous editions this was followed by a bunch of sample rotes. The sample rotes can be illustrative, but use up a lot of space without a lot of explanation of how things work. M20 shoved all those sample effects into a simple to reference set of charts to cover common effects that works just as well if not better than the sample rotes do. How Do You Do That? is a bit of a contentious subject around here, but a lot of that is disagreeing with the answers being good ones more than if the book does the job of really digging into the idea of how to work the Spheres.<br /> <br /> I think the biggest issue against M20 for the intended game is that M20 is <b>huge</b> and the vast majority of the book is going to be tossed out. As with the rest of the X20 core books, M20 has a large and comprehensive discussion of factions both big and small within the official groups, lots of discussions of the setting and metaplot, and so on. It's a big book, and an expensive book, that you're probably going to end up ignoring around half of the content. But it's hard to say if really nice things M20 does (something that wasn't brought up is that M20 actually acknowledges and takes a position on the HAP vs HOP/RBD vs. PBD debates), are worth it anyway. There is a M20 Quickstart that might be worth looking at first as a much smaller investment into seeing what M20 is like before grabbing the tome of smiting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T10:31:41-07:00","id":1101673,"title":"","content":"I dont recommend M20, it is a poorly and unprofessional written book that seriously lacked any editorial supervision. Its longer that it needed and wasted word count it supposedly didnt have in stroking the authors ego.<br /> <br /> As a backer of this book i deeply regret giving my money for this &quot;book&quot;. Phil Brucatto is the rob liefeld to his mage\u00b4s deadpool. Anything good that came from Mage was done by more professional and better writers that took his ideas and made them something brilliant and i regret not knowing the poor quality of his work before i backed m20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 06-08-2017, 01:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T10:45:48-07:00","id":1101678,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">something that wasn't brought up is that M20 actually acknowledges and takes a position on the HAP vs HOP/RBD vs. PBD debates</div> </div> </div> </div>YES, THIS is really something. M20 has much more clear rules about what's Coincident and what's Vulgar. This deserves attention<br /> <br /> The amount of discussion in this forum, and others, about that issue that was answered by the canonization of that fan-made model (HAP) it's simply astounding.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T15:17:27-07:00","id":1101755,"title":"","content":"Many thanks for all the helpful replies.<br /> <br /> I have paged through the quickstart PDF. It's actually what made me consider buying M20 at all.<br /> <br /> I have attempted to run Mage for two of my players in the past, and the big issues for them were things like paradigm and foci use. A little more beef around that would probably save me some gray hairs.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I do not need all that fluff, though a breakdown of the various metaplot elements could give me some material. I'm repurposing the Avatar Storm, for example.<br /> <br /> As a follow-up, would it be viable for me to purchase one copy for myself, and allow my players to rely on my notes and Revised books? Mage doesn't have as many moving parts as Exalted, or even Vampire, really.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T15:47:39-07:00","id":1101772,"title":"","content":"I think you could make one copy of M20 + a bunch of copies of Revised could work if include a pages of charts from M20 for the players.<br /> <br /> The amount of moving parts didn't change significantly. Multiple actions are like the rest of the main X20 games. The Ability list change slightly but people would have their character sheets for that. They added Vidare, which isn't really a moving part, it just adds a bit to paradigm as it relates to the Umbra. There's a few changes to some of the Backgrounds, but you're probably not going to feel that from just Revised Core during play (some of the bigger changes are going to be during char-gen and XP spending). There's a few small differences in specific effects and what dot rating they require in Spheres (but that's all in the summary chart). Paradox is probably the biggest change that would mess up players a bit if they're looking at Revised's core, since the default M20 version is a mix of 2e and Rev. on that (of course you should be using Malcolm Shepard's clarification/errata for that anyway which never got printed). There's a lot of combat maneuvers, and equipment, that aren't in Rev. core, but again printing out a bunch of charts. Combat also has a major change in it, though it's actually a reduction of moving parts: M20 dropped the declaration phase in combat.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T17:28:57-07:00","id":1101805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1101620-m20-worth-it-for-my-game?p=1101772#post1101772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you could make one copy of M20 + a bunch of copies of Revised could work if include a pages of charts from M20 for the players.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did this for my group, two physical copies of M20 plus PDF and then I printed out the various charts and stapled them to the character sheet in addition to a small page of snippets on focus and tools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T19:18:12-07:00","id":1101833,"title":"","content":"I would personally recommend M20 for the Focus section alone. Unlike previous editions, it actually explains how mages can see the world, what their general methods are for tinkering with it, and what tools they use to do that (Paradigm, Practice, and Instruments, respectively). It's all modular and thematic, and quite extensive. It even goes into how the same Paradigms and Practices can differ wildly, depending on which cultural and esoteric tradition one subscribes to.<br /> <br /> If you're copying sections of the text and printing them out, considering making this part available to everyone. It'll make explaining what magick is about more streamlined, while letting your group pick and choose elements that appeal to them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-09T05:08:31-07:00","id":1101931,"title":"","content":"Personally, I've made it no secret that I prefer to houserule revised rather than attempt to make use of M20.<br /> <br /> One thing I will say, M20 is a lot better about the mundane skills side of mage, while revised handles magic better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-09T06:17:58-07:00","id":1101946,"title":"","content":"Thanks to everyone for the helpful replies. It seems worth it to me to invest in the PDF and see how I feel about it. I'm a bit leery of huge physical books after Ex3.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-09T10:55:10-07:00","id":1102040,"title":"","content":"I have both physical book and pdf and for me, personally, I find the pdf much more useful.<br /> <br /> The book is lovely, but it's a bit too big for practical use. There is just too much to sift through. The pdf on the other hand is searchable, hyperlinked and indexed.<br /> <br /> Though there is something to be said for the dramatic factor of plopping a giant physical book in front of your players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-09T12:26:39-07:00","id":1102070,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1101620-m20-worth-it-for-my-game?p=1102040#post1102040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have both physical book and pdf and for me, personally, I find the pdf much more useful.<br /> <br /> The book is lovely, but it's a bit too big for practical use. There is just too much to sift through. The pdf on the other hand is searchable, hyperlinked and indexed.<br /> <br /> Though there is something to be said for the dramatic factor of plopping a giant physical book in front of your players.</div> </div> </div> </div>One of my selling points for Mage is &quot;fewer rules than Exalted,&quot; so the plopping won't be that helpful. Hyper-linked PDF sounds pretty swank, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}